==English==
[[File:Dziennik Mod Paryskich 15.jpg|thumb|fashion plate with men's and women's styles (1845)]]

===Etymology===
Initially produced as [[engraving]]s.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-fashion plate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[picture]], usually a full-page [[advertisement]], showing the latest fashion in [[clothing]].
#* {{RQ:Henry Waifs and Strays|chapter=Confessions of a Humorist|passage=By turning the skirt and adding a ruffle of rhyme you would hardly recognize it as ''vers de societe'' with neatly shod feet and a '''fashion-plate''' illustration.}}
#* {{RQ:Wharton Age of Innocence|passage=He was a pure man of letters, untimely born in a world that had no need of letters; {{...|but after publishing one volume of brief and exquisite literary appreciations, of which one hundred and twenty copies were sold, thirty given away, and the balance eventually destroyed by the publishers (as per contract) to make room for more marketable material,}} he had abandoned his real calling, and taken a sub-editorial job on a women's weekly, where '''fashion-plates''' and paper patterns alternated with New England love-stories and advertisements of temperance drinks.}}
#* {{RQ:Lewis Main Street|passage=Nat Hicks's Tailor Shop, on a side street off Main. A one-story building. A '''fashion-plate''' showing human pitchforks in garments which looked as hard as steel plate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=April Calahan|title=Fashion Plates: 150 Years of Style|publisher=Yale University Press|isbn=9780300212266|page=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PfCACgAAQBAJ&pg=PA1&dq=%22fashion+plates%22&hl=&cd=1&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22fashion%20plates%22&f=false|text=Today, in an age when the promotion of fashion is dominated by social media, video, and photography, '''fashion plates''' are nearly forgotten—relics of history consulted by few aside from professional costume designers and scholars of history.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A person who dresses in especially [[stylish]] fashions.
#: {{ux|en|I'm no '''fashion plate''', and don't dress to impress.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Beyond the City|chapter=Breaking the ice|passage=In their stiff, crackling dresses of black silk, with jet-bespangled jackets, and little rows of cylindrical grey curls drooping down on either side of their black bonnets, they looked like two old '''fashion plates''' which had wandered off into the wrong decade.}}

